When multiple migrations are added in one PR, sort by numeric prefix rather than lexicographic filename order to avoid execution sequence errors.
When adding two or more database migration files in a single pull request where later migrations depend on earlier ones.
## The Problem
Many Go-based migration runners (like the one in this codebase) use `sort.Strings()` on file names to determine execution order. This relies on lexicographic sorting, which means "100_" sorts before "99_" because '1' < '9'. If you add two migrations in one PR (e.g., 99_add_feature.sql and 100_backfill_feature.sql), the backfill may run before the schema change, causing failures.

## The Fix
Instead of renaming files to zero-padded names (which risks re-running all migrations if the runner records by filename), modify the runner to sort by the numeric prefix extracted from the filename.

Example implementation:
```go
import (
    "os"
    "strconv"
    "strings"
)

func pending(dir string, applied map[string]bool) ([]string, error) {
    entries, err := os.ReadDir(dir)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    var names []string
    for _, e := range entries {
        if !strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), ".sql") || applied[e.Name()] {
            continue
        }
        names = append(names, e.Name())
    }
    // Sort by numeric prefix instead of lexicographic order
    sort.Slice(names, func(i, j int) bool { return prefix(names[i]) < prefix(names[j]) })
    return names, nil
}

func prefix(name string) int {
    n, err := strconv.Atoi(strings.SplitN(name, "_", 2)[0])
    if err != nil {
        panic("migration without a numeric prefix: " + name)
    }
    return n
}
```

## Why not rename files?
The runner records applied migrations by filename in `schema_migrations`. Renaming existing files (e.g., 99_ to 0099_) would make them appear unapplied, causing all migrations to re-run on staging/production. Sorting numerically avoids this side effect.
